1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,303
In the next few minutes you will learn the difference between blade guards and blade soakers, and a ninety second routine that stops your short track blades from rusting.

2
00:00:11,303 --> 00:00:14,760
You will also learn how to read rust once it starts.

3
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:19,415
For most skaters, the blades are the most expensive part of the setup.

4
00:00:19,415 --> 00:00:23,737
And the thing that kills them is rarely a crash or a bad sharpen.

5
00:00:23,737 --> 00:00:24,535
It is water.

6
00:00:24,535 --> 00:00:31,450
A thin film of melted ice, left on ground steel inside a closed bag, is enough to start pitting an edge.

7
00:00:31,450 --> 00:00:37,966
Once rust bites into the running surface, you have to grind metal away to get back to clean steel.

8
00:00:37,966 --> 00:00:41,290
Every sharpen after that takes life off the blade.

9
00:00:41,990 --> 00:00:44,326
Here is why it happens so quickly.

10
00:00:44,326 --> 00:00:48,174
When you skate, the edge melts a very thin layer of ice.

11
00:00:48,174 --> 00:00:50,097
That is what lets you glide.

12
00:00:50,097 --> 00:00:54,975
When you step off, that water is still sitting on freshly ground steel.

13
00:00:54,975 --> 00:00:59,441
Blade steel is hardened to hold an edge, not to resist corrosion.

14
00:00:59,441 --> 00:01:02,533
So it reacts easily with moisture and oxygen.

15
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:04,251
Two things speed this up.

16
00:01:04,251 --> 00:01:05,487
The first is grit.

17
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:12,083
Dust, floor debris and stray fibres hold water against the steel long after it would have dried.

18
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:14,075
The second is a sealed space.

19
00:01:14,075 --> 00:01:18,541
Zip damp skates inside a bag, and the moisture has nowhere to go.

20
00:01:18,541 --> 00:01:21,633
The air around the blade stays wet for hours.

21
00:01:22,333 --> 00:01:26,595
This diagram shows a blade in cross section, just after a session.

22
00:01:26,595 --> 00:01:31,246
Along the top of the steel there is a thin film of water, shown in blue.

23
00:01:31,246 --> 00:01:34,862
Mixed in with it are small grey specks of grit and dust.

24
00:01:34,862 --> 00:01:40,094
Down at the bottom is the edge, and that is where the orange spots of rust begin.

25
00:01:40,094 --> 00:01:45,196
The edge is the thinnest part of the steel, so it is the first part to corrode.

26
00:01:45,196 --> 00:01:52,042
That is the worst place for rust to start, because the edge is the part that does all the work on the ice.

27
00:01:52,742 --> 00:01:55,421
Hard guards are the rigid plastic covers.

28
00:01:55,421 --> 00:01:59,668
They have one job, which is to protect your edges while you walk.

29
00:01:59,668 --> 00:02:03,784
Rink floors, rubber matting, tiles and concrete all chew steel.

30
00:02:03,784 --> 00:02:09,926
One unguarded step onto a hard floor can put a nick in an edge that you will feel all session.

31
00:02:09,926 --> 00:02:15,153
So put your guards on the moment you step off the ice, before you walk anywhere.

32
00:02:15,153 --> 00:02:18,943
Take them off at the barrier, not halfway across the room.

33
00:02:18,943 --> 00:02:24,496
Now and then, wipe out the inside of the guard, because grit collects in the channel.

34
00:02:24,496 --> 00:02:29,789
Check that the spring still holds firm, because a slack guard drops off mid walk.

35
00:02:29,789 --> 00:02:32,271
And never store your skates in guards.

36
00:02:32,972 --> 00:02:37,435
Soakers are the soft fabric covers, and they do the opposite job.

37
00:02:37,435 --> 00:02:39,769
They are for storage, not walking.

38
00:02:39,769 --> 00:02:45,400
The terry cloth inside wicks water off the blade and holds it away from the steel.

39
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:48,764
A hard guard seals moisture in against the metal.

40
00:02:48,764 --> 00:02:51,374
A soaker draws it out and lets it dry.

41
00:02:51,374 --> 00:02:56,111
The StayBent Blade Soakers come in two sizes, chosen by blade length.

42
00:02:56,111 --> 00:03:00,300
Small and medium fits blades from thirteen to sixteen inches.

43
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:04,420
Medium and large fits sixteen and a half to eighteen inches.

44
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,166
Measure your own blade before you order.

45
00:03:07,166 --> 00:03:12,660
And remember, soakers give no protection on a hard floor, so never walk in them.

46
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:15,287
Here are the two side by side.

47
00:03:15,287 --> 00:03:18,628
On the left, in green, are hard guards, for walking.

48
00:03:18,628 --> 00:03:22,741
The rigid shell takes the abuse of floors, matting and concrete.

49
00:03:22,741 --> 00:03:27,881
But if you store your skates in them, they trap water, so take them off at home.

50
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:30,965
On the right, in blue, are soakers, for the bag.

51
00:03:30,965 --> 00:03:35,014
The terry cloth pulls moisture off the steel and holds it away.

52
00:03:35,014 --> 00:03:39,511
Use them for storage and transport, and dry them out between sessions.

53
00:03:39,511 --> 00:03:42,981
Most skaters carry both, and swap over at the barrier.

54
00:03:42,981 --> 00:03:45,230
One without the other leaves a gap.

55
00:03:45,930 --> 00:03:49,815
This is the routine that protects your steel, in five steps.

56
00:03:49,815 --> 00:03:58,362
Do it every time, even after a short session, because twenty minutes on the ice leaves just as much water on the blade as two hours.

57
00:03:58,362 --> 00:04:00,629
Step one, guards on at the barrier.

58
00:04:00,629 --> 00:04:03,219
Do not take a single step on bare steel.

59
00:04:03,219 --> 00:04:05,938
Step two, sit down and take the boots off.

60
00:04:05,938 --> 00:04:09,888
Step three, pull the guards off and wipe the whole blade dry.

61
00:04:09,888 --> 00:04:14,291
Do the running surface, both sides, the cups and the mounting bolts.

62
00:04:14,291 --> 00:04:17,075
Water hides in the corners around the cups.

63
00:04:17,075 --> 00:04:21,219
Step four, let the steel air for a few minutes while you change.

64
00:04:21,219 --> 00:04:23,421
Step five, soakers on for the bag.

65
00:04:24,121 --> 00:04:26,487
The routine does not end at the rink.

66
00:04:26,487 --> 00:04:31,285
When you get home, take the skates out of the bag and pull the soakers off.

67
00:04:31,285 --> 00:04:35,826
Let both the blades and the soakers dry fully before your next session.

68
00:04:35,826 --> 00:04:38,257
This step matters more than it sounds.

69
00:04:38,257 --> 00:04:44,525
A soaker that stays damp all the time holds moisture against the steel instead of drawing it away.

70
00:04:44,525 --> 00:04:48,235
That turns your best blade care habit into your worst one.

71
00:04:48,235 --> 00:04:52,073
While you are drying the blade, you can also check the edge.

72
00:04:52,073 --> 00:04:56,422
Run a fingertip lightly along the side of the edge, never across it.

73
00:04:56,422 --> 00:05:03,010
If you feel a small raised lip, called a burr, a few light passes with a deburr stone will clean it up.

74
00:05:03,710 --> 00:05:06,069
Not all rust is the same problem.

75
00:05:06,069 --> 00:05:10,500
It runs through three stages, and this diagram shows each one.

76
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:13,073
Stage one, in green, is a grey haze.

77
00:05:13,073 --> 00:05:18,148
Bright steel has gone dull and cloudy, but nothing has been eaten away.

78
00:05:18,148 --> 00:05:20,220
Wipe it dry and keep skating.

79
00:05:20,220 --> 00:05:22,222
Do not start grinding at it.

80
00:05:22,222 --> 00:05:24,723
Stage two, in orange, is speckling.

81
00:05:24,723 --> 00:05:28,297
Fine orange dots, often near the edge or the cups.

82
00:05:28,297 --> 00:05:28,869
Act now.

83
00:05:28,869 --> 00:05:36,302
Work along the blade with the fine side of a combination stone, using light, even pressure, then deburr.

84
00:05:36,302 --> 00:05:38,589
Stage three, in red, is pitting.

85
00:05:38,589 --> 00:05:41,519
These are craters in the running surface.

86
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:48,738
Only a deep grind removes them, so take the blade to a technician and expect to lose some blade life.

87
00:05:49,438 --> 00:05:52,133
Skaters in India face an extra problem.

88
00:05:52,133 --> 00:05:55,864
You step out of a cold, dry rink into warm, humid air.

89
00:05:55,864 --> 00:06:02,843
Condensation forms on cold steel almost at once, just like a cold bottle sweating on a hot afternoon.

90
00:06:02,843 --> 00:06:07,266
The blade can be dry when you pack it and wet ten minutes later.

91
00:06:07,266 --> 00:06:09,477
So change when you dry, not how.

92
00:06:09,477 --> 00:06:17,354
Wipe the blades before you leave the building, fit soakers, then wipe again once everything has warmed up at home.

93
00:06:17,354 --> 00:06:22,536
In the monsoon, and at coastal rinks, treat that second wipe as compulsory.

94
00:06:22,536 --> 00:06:27,304
Store skates in an air conditioned room, not a cupboard or a balcony.

95
00:06:27,304 --> 00:06:31,450
When you travel, blades go in soakers, never in hard guards.

96
00:06:31,450 --> 00:06:37,254
Open the bag as soon as you arrive, and carry a spare pair of soakers on long trips.

97
00:06:37,954 --> 00:06:40,176
Guards and soakers both wear out.

98
00:06:40,176 --> 00:06:48,594
A guard is finished when the spring no longer holds it firm, when the shell cracks, or when the blade sits loose and rattles.

99
00:06:48,594 --> 00:06:55,530
A soaker is finished when the cloth goes thin, matted or hard, or holds a smell that will not wash out.

100
00:06:55,530 --> 00:06:59,233
Hand wash soakers in cool water and air dry them fully.

101
00:06:59,233 --> 00:07:00,849
A few mistakes to avoid.

102
00:07:00,849 --> 00:07:06,169
Do not leave skates in the boot of a car, where heat and humidity keep cycling.

103
00:07:06,169 --> 00:07:09,873
Do not dry the blade with a damp cloth or a used towel.

104
00:07:09,873 --> 00:07:12,499
Do not forget the cups, lips and bolts.

105
00:07:12,499 --> 00:07:19,233
And always wipe along the blade, not across the edge, because that is how skaters cut their fingers.

106
00:07:19,933 --> 00:07:22,685
If you remember one thing, make it this.

107
00:07:22,685 --> 00:07:24,267
Guards are for walking.

108
00:07:24,267 --> 00:07:25,919
Soakers are for storage.

109
00:07:25,919 --> 00:07:33,212
Swap them over at the barrier, wipe the steel dry every single time, and let everything dry fully at home.

110
00:07:33,212 --> 00:07:40,711
Ninety seconds of habit is what separates blades that last for years from blades that pit in a single season.

111
00:07:40,711 --> 00:07:48,692
The full guide, with the rust stages, the frequently asked questions and the soaker sizing, is on the LiveLica blog.

112
00:07:48,692 --> 00:07:50,825
The link is in the description.
